\noindent \textbf{Summary}\\
\textbf{1.} While animal population studies heavily rely on tracking data to quantify space use and habitat selection, the realities of animal-borne telemetry often yield temporally-irregular location data subject to measurement error. \\
\textbf{2.} We introduce \verb|langevinSSM|, an R package that implements solutions to several longstanding challenges when attempting to infer habitat (or resource) selection and utilization distributions from animal location data. It provides a single-stage model fitting framework for telemetry data that are subject to location measurement error (e.g., Argos, Fastloc GPS, GPS), temporally irregular (i.e., continuous-time) sampling, missing data, and/or spatial constraints (e.g., coastlines for marine or terrestrial animals). \\
\textbf{3.} \verb|langevinSSM| efficiently fits a state-space formulation of the habitat-driven Langevin diffusion via maximum likelihood methods by simultaneously integrating over the true locations while accounting for the (unobserved) spatial habitat covariates encountered along those latent paths. Spatial constraints are incorporated directly into the dynamics of the Langevin diffusion, thereby alleviating the need for pre- or post-processing to account for observed or estimated true locations that reside in restricted habitat. Compared to simulation-based methods (e.g., MCMC, particle filter), model fitting with \verb|langevinSSM| is exceptionally fast (e.g., 14 seconds for 25,000 observations on a modern laptop).\\
\textbf{4.} We provide a brief overview of the Langevin diffusion state-space model, describe the main features and workflow of the package (e.g., data formatting, model fitting, model checking and diagnostics, simulation), and demonstrate its use with real and simulated tracking data. \\

\noindent \textbf{Key-words} biologging, biotelemetry, continuous time, habitat selection, Langevin diffusion, state-space model, utilization distribution

\section{Introduction}

Animal movement is central to many ecological processes \citep[e.g.,][]{nathan2008movement,gomez2025understanding}. By quantifying how individuals select habitat and distribute themselves across landscapes, researchers can address broader challenges in population ecology, conservation, and management \citep[e.g.,][]{boyce1999relating,morales2010building,allen2016linking}. Alongside recent breakthroughs in biologging technology, there has been a surge in analytical methods designed to infer animal movement, behavior, space use, and resource selection from telemetry data \citep[e.g.,][]{hooten2017animal,merkle2021emerging}. Despite these advances, the realities of animal-borne telemetry often yield temporally-irregular location data subject to measurement error \citep[especially in aquatic environments; e.g.,][]{costa2010accuracy}, yet standard resource selection methods typically require temporally regular locations and ignore location uncertainty \citep[e.g.,][]{thurfjell2014applications,signer2019animal}. Single-stage approaches for estimating habitat selection within a continuous-time framework that accounts for observation error and/or missing data has traditionally required novel statistical methods and custom model-fitting algorithms that are not practical for many practitioners.

Recently, \citet{dupont2026inferring} introduced a continuous-time state-space model (SSM) for inferring habitat selection and utilization distributions (UDs) from animal location data subject to measurement error, temporally irregular sampling, and missing data. The model relies on the habitat-driven Langevin diffusion \citep{michelot2019langevin,michelot2024multiscale}, which is a continuous-time stochastic process that describes the movement of an animal as it responds to spatially-varying environmental covariates (e.g., elevation, slope, bathymetry). %The Langevin diffusion SSM simultaneously accounts for observation error, bridges temporally irregular observations, and imputes missing data while inferring habitat selection gradients driving the movement process.
While we defer readers to the source material for the specific details of the stochastic differential equations, the key feature of the Langevin diffusion is that the position \citep[in the ``overdamped'' case;][]{michelot2019langevin} or velocity \citep[in the ``underdamped'' case;][]{michelot2024multiscale} process includes a drift term that is proportional to the gradient of the log of the utilization distribution, $\nabla \log \pi(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t)$, where $\nabla$ is the gradient operator, $\boldsymbol{\mu}_t$ is the location at time $t$, and $\pi(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t)$ is the utilization distribution evaluated at $\boldsymbol{\mu}_t$. The utilization distribution is modeled using the standard form of a resource selection function \citep[RSF;][]{manly2002resource}:
\begin{equation}
\pi(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t) = \frac{\exp(\mathbf{c}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t)^\top \boldsymbol{\beta})}{\int \exp(\mathbf{c}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t)^\top \boldsymbol{\beta}) \, d\boldsymbol{\mu}_t},
\label{eq:ud}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{c}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t)$ is a vector of $K$ spatial covariates evaluated at location $\boldsymbol{\mu}_t$ and $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ is a $K$-vector of corresponding habitat selection coefficients. The estimated utilization distribution can therefore be derived solely from the estimated habitat selection coefficients without any need for simulation as is typically required when using standard resource selection methods \citep[e.g.,][]{signer2024simulating}. Furthermore, because the model is formulated in continuous time, the movement parameters are time-scale invariant and therefore more easily comparable across studies than when using discrete-time approaches \citep[e.g.,][]{avgar2016integrated,blackwell2026consistent}

When location data are subject to measurement error, both the true locations $(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t)$ and the spatial covariates $\left(\mathbf{c}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t)\right)$ encountered along the latent path are unobserved. We instead observe a set of telemetry locations $(\mathbf{y}_t)$ that are related to the true locations via a measurement error model, typically assumed to be of the form:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{y}_t \mid \boldsymbol{\mu}_t \sim \text{Normal}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_t),
\label{eq:me}
\end{equation}
where $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_t$ is the (often assumed known) measurement error covariance matrix at time $t$ \citep[e.g.,][]{Johnson2008,mcclintock2015modelling}. This presents a significant challenge for estimating the habitat selection parameters $(\boldsymbol{\beta})$, the diffusion (or ``speed'') parameter $(\sigma)$, and, for the underdamped model, the friction (or autocorrelation) parameter $(\gamma)$, as the likelihood function now requires integrating over all possible true locations and associated covariates. \citet{dupont2026inferring} developed a fast and efficient maximum likelihood approach for fitting the Langevin diffusion SSM using the Template Model Builder (\verb|TMB|) R package \citep{kristensen2016tmb}. Given the observed $\mathbf{y}_t$ (and any missing but desirable $\boldsymbol{\mu}_t$), their approach relies on the Laplace approximation to simultaneously integrate over the true locations while accounting for the (unobserved) spatial habitat covariates. This represents a potentially significant advance over simulation-based approaches (e.g., Markov chain Monte Carlo, particle filter) that are often computationally intensive and slow to converge \citep[e.g.,][]{bolker2013strategies,Albertsen2015}.

Here we introduce an R package, \verb|langevinSSM|, for inferences about habitat selection and utilization distributions from irregular and error-prone animal tracking data. In addition to fitting the Langevin diffusion SSM, the package provides functions for simulating from the model, predicting locations bridging irregular observations, calculating and visualizing utilization distributions, and assessing model fit. The package also accommodates spatial constraints (e.g., coastlines for marine or terrestrial animals) by incorporating them directly into the dynamics of the Langevin diffusion \citep[\textit{sensu}][]{delporte2025spatial}, thereby alleviating the need for pre- or post-processing to account for observed or estimated true locations that reside in restricted habitat. The package also implements the conditionally independent hierarchical modelling approach of \citet{johnson2022greater} for inferring population-level habitat selection and utilization distributions from multiple individuals.

In the material that follows, we provide a brief overview of the package, describe its main features and workflows (e.g., data formatting, model fitting, model checking and diagnostics, simulation), and demonstrate its use with real and simulated tracking data. The package is available on CRAN (\url{https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=langevinSSM}) and GitHub (\url{https://github.com/bmcclintock/langevinSSM}).  \verb|langevinSSM| is designed to be user-friendly and accessible to ecologists and wildlife biologists, while also providing advanced functionality for statisticians and quantitative ecologists.

% allows discretization bias to be minimize by padding the data with NAs (e.g. at regular intervals) or using the approach of Blackwell and Matthiopoulos (2024)

\section{langevinSSM overview}

The workflow for \verb|langevinSSM| analyses consists of data preparation, model specification, model fitting, model checking, and simulation. Figure \ref{fig:workflow} illustrates the workflow, and Table \ref{tab:functions} summarizes the primary workhorse functions. Throughout this section, we will use simulated data consisting of three tracks (\verb|rawData|) and a list of four simulated habitat covariates (\verb|exampleCovs|; Fig.\ \ref{fig:covPlot}) to demonstrate key concepts and workflows:

<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE, size="small">>=
head(rawData)

summary(rawData)
@
\noindent where \verb|id| is the animal ID, \verb|date| is the timestamp, \verb|x| is the (projected) easting coordinate, \verb|y| is the (projected) northing coordinate, and \verb|smaj|, \verb|smin|, and \verb|eor| correspond to the error ellipse semi-major axis, semi-minor axis, and orientation (in degrees from north), respectively.

