\subsection{Data preparation}
Location measurement error is ubiquitous in animal-borne telemetry, and its severity is driven by the specific tracking device as well as the characteristics of the study system \citep[e.g.,][]{jerde2005gps,costa2010accuracy,frair2010resolving,mcclintock2015modelling}. Because measurement error can come in many different forms, the \verb|formatData| function converts raw animal tracking data---provided as either a standard data frame or an \verb|sf| \citep{pebesma2018simple} spatial object---into a standard form (a \verb|dataLangevin| object) required for downstream model fitting using the \verb|fitLangevin| function. %Possible forms of measurement error include Argos least squares (i.e., location quality classes), Argos error ellipses, and generic bivariate normal error (e.g., GPS or Fastloc GPS).

Because the Langevin diffusion SSM is in continuous time and accommodates various forms of measurement error, the \verb|formatData| function performs several purposes, including:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Coordinate and temporal standardization:} The function requires coordinates to be in a projected metric coordinate system, rather than unprojected longitude and latitude. It calculates the time step (\verb|dt|) between consecutive observations based on a user-specified time unit, such as hours.

  \item \textbf{Location measurement error:} The function accepts Argos error ellipses defined by a semi-major axis, semi-minor axis, and orientation \citep[e.g.][]{mcclintock2015modelling}. It also accepts $x$- and $y$-axis standard deviations for generic GPS or Fastloc GPS error. For telemetry locations that lack explicit measurement error data but contain a location quality class (such as Argos least squares classes 3, 2, 1, 0, A, B, or Z), users can supply an error multiplication factor (EMF) table to specify the standard deviations for each location quality class via the \verb|emf| argument. If no explicit measurement error data or EMF table is provided, the function assumes the observations are measured without error.

  \item \textbf{Custom prediction times:} Researchers often need to standardize the temporal resolution of an irregular track or estimate an animal's location at specific, unobserved time points. Through the \verb|predTimes| argument, \verb|formatData| can pad the dataset with these custom timestamps, assigning \verb|NA| to the corresponding coordinates and measurement error data. During model fitting (or after model fitting via the \verb|predLangevin| function), the observation error evaluation for these specific time steps is bypassed, and estimation of the corresponding true locations relies entirely on the Langevin diffusion. By filling gaps with additional prediction times, \verb|predTimes| can also be used to help reduce potential discretization bias when time steps (\verb|dt|) are relatively large (see Section \ref{sec:model}).
\end{itemize}

When calling \verb|formatData|, the column names for the animal ID, timestamp, coordinates, and measurement error data are identified, as well as a time unit (e.g., hours, days) for calculating the time step between observations (\verb|dt|):

<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE, size="small">>=
# Format the telemetry data
formatDat <- formatData(data = rawData,
                        id = "id",
                        date = "date",
                        coord = c("x", "y"),
                        epar = c("smaj", "smin", "eor"),
                        time.unit = "hours")

head(formatDat)
@
\noindent Note that the location quality (\verb|lc|) and standard deviation error (\verb|x.err| and \verb|y.err|) fields are \verb|NA| because these tracks only include error ellipse data.

\subsection{Model specification and fitting}
\label{sec:model}
The \verb|fitLangevin| function fits the habitat-driven Langevin diffusion state-space model to data prepared by \verb|formatData| (or simulated by \verb|simLangevin|) using maximum likelihood methods. The continuous-time process is approximated using an Euler discretization scheme, where the approximation becomes more accurate as the time step (\verb|dt|) decreases. Using the \verb|model| argument, the underdamped \citep[][]{michelot2024multiscale} or overdamped \citep{michelot2019langevin} model can be specified. Habitat covariates are specified via the \verb|spatialCovs| argument. \verb|spatialCovs| must be a list of $K$ \verb|SpatRaster| objects created with the \verb|rast| function in R package \verb|terra| \citep{hijmans2026terra}. By representing the covariates as rasters, the \verb|langevinSSM| package relies on discrete-space approximations for calculating the covariate gradients (using bilinear interpolation) and the utilization distribution (Eq.\ \ref{eq:ud}).

\subsubsection{Movement parameters}
The movement parameters to be estimated include the $K$ habitat selection coefficients (\verb|beta|), the diffusion (or speed) parameter (\verb|sigma|), and, for the underdamped model, the friction (or autocorrelation) parameter (\verb|gamma|). For each element of \verb|spatialCovs|, a corresponding \verb|beta| is estimated during model fitting, where $\beta_k>0$ indicates the animal tends to move toward higher values of the covariate, $\beta_k<0$ indicates the animal tends to move toward lower values of the covariate, and $\beta_k=0$ indicates no movement response to the covariate. When the observed locations include measurement error, the true locations (\verb|mu|) are treated as random effects. For the underdamped model, the true velocities (\verb|vel|) are also treated as random effects.

\subsubsection{Observation parameters}
\verb|fitLangevin| handles measurement error through two distinct internal models with the same underlying structure, which differ in how the observation error covariance matrix $(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_t)$ is constructed based on the available error information for each observation $\mathbf{y}_t$. The \verb|obs_dist| argument can be used to choose between two distributions for the observation model, the standard bivariate Gaussian (normal) distribution (\verb|obs_dist = "Gaussian"|):
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{y}_t \mid \boldsymbol{\mu}_t \sim \text{Normal}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_t),
\end{equation}
or, to accommodate heavier tails and reduce the influence of outliers, the bivariate t-distribution (\verb|obs_dist = "t"|):
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{y}_t \mid \boldsymbol{\mu}_t \sim t(\boldsymbol{\mu}_t, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_t, \nu).
\end{equation}
When the t-distribution is selected, the degrees of freedom $\nu$ (\verb|df|) is included as an additional parameter that can be estimated or fixed using the \verb|map| argument.

For the error ellipse model, the observation error covariance matrix is derived as:
\begin{equation}
\label{Sigma}
\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_t = \begin{bmatrix}
\sigma_{1,{t}}^2 & \sigma_{12,{t}} \\
\sigma_{21,{t}} & \sigma_{2,{t}}^2
\end{bmatrix}, \qquad \text{with} \qquad
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
\sigma_{1,t}^2 &= \left(\frac{M_t}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2 \sin^2 r_t + \left(\frac{m_t\psi}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2 \cos^2 r_t, \\
\sigma_{2,t}^2 &= \left(\frac{M_t}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2 \cos^2 r_t + \left(\frac{m_t\psi}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2 \sin^2 r_t, \\
\sigma_{12,t} &= \sigma_{21,t} = \frac{(M_t^2 - m_t^2\psi^2)}{2} \cos(r_t)\sin(r_t),
\end{aligned}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $M_t$ is the semi-major axis, $m_t$ is the semi-minor axis, $r_t$ is the orientation of the ellipse in radians \citep{mcclintock2015modelling}, and $\psi$ (\verb|psi|) is a scaling parameter that can be optionally estimated (otherwise $\psi=1$). For the standard deviation model, the observation error covariance matrix is:
\begin{equation}
  \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_t =
  \begin{bmatrix}
  \tau_1^2 \sigma_{1,t}^2 & \rho_o \tau_1 \tau_2 \sigma_{1,t} \sigma_{2,t}\\ \rho_o \tau_1 \tau_2 \sigma_{1,t} \sigma_{2,t} & \tau_2^2 \sigma_{2,t}^2
  \end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}
where $\tau_1$ (\verb|tau_1|) and $\tau_2$ (\verb|tau_2|) are scaling parameters that can be optionally estimated (otherwise $\tau_1=\tau_2=1$), and $\rho_o$ (\verb|rho_o|) is a correlation parameter that can be optionally estimated (otherwise $\rho_o=0$). Users can specify initial values for all parameters and random effects using the \verb|par| argument. Any parameters that are not included in \verb|par| are initialized as in Table \ref{tab:initial_values}. Any parameters or random effects can be fixed using the \verb|map| argument.

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Default initial parameter and random effect values used by the \texttt{fitLangevin} function when not specified via the \texttt{par} argument, where $R_t^2$ is the squared displacement between consecutive observations and $\Delta_t$ is the time step. If any observed locations $(\mathbf{y}_t)$ are missing (i.e., \texttt{NA}), the corresponding $\boldsymbol{\mu}_t$ are initialized using linear interpolation.}
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
  \toprule
  Parameter & Description & Initial Value \\
  \midrule
  \verb|beta| & Habitat selection coefficients
  & K-vector of zeros \\
  \verb|sigma| & Diffusion (or speed) parameter
  & $\sqrt{\text{mean}(R_t^2 / (2 \Delta_t))}$ \\
  \verb|gamma|\textsuperscript{a} & Friction (or autocorrelation) parameter
  & $1 / \text{median}(\Delta_t)$ \\
  \verb|mu| & True locations
  & Observed locations $(\mathbf{y})$ \\
  \verb|vel|\textsuperscript{a} & True velocities
  & Matrix of zeros \\
  \verb|psi| & Error ellipse scaling factor
  & $1$ \\
  \verb|tau| & Standard deviation scaling factors
  & 2-vector of ones \\
  \verb|rho_o| & Standard deviation correlation parameter
  & $0$ \\
  \verb|df|\textsuperscript{b} & Degrees of freedom parameter
  & $5$ \\
  \bottomrule
  \multicolumn{3}{l}{\textsuperscript{a}\small{ Underdamped Langevin model only}}\\
  \multicolumn{3}{l}{\textsuperscript{b}\small{ t-distribution observation model only}}\\
  \end{tabular}
  \label{tab:initial_values}
\end{table}

When calling \verb|fitLangevin|, it will first check that the data and covariate rasters are in the correct format before fitting the specified model using maximum likelihood methods. After fitting, it returns a \verb|fitLangevin| object containing the estimated parameters, random effects, and associated standard errors. The \verb|plot.fitLangevin| function can be used to visualize the fitted model, including the estimated utilization distribution:
<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE, cache=TRUE>>=
fit <- fitLangevin(data = formatDat,
                   model = "underdamped",
                   spatialCovs = exampleCovs)

summary(fit)

plot(fit, data = formatDat, spatialCovs = exampleCovs)
@
\noindent The \verb|getUD| function calculates and returns the utilization distribution (and its uncertainty) from the estimated habitat selection coefficients as a \verb|SpatRaster| object, which can be used for further plotting and analysis. The \verb|rasterOverlap| function calculates the overlap between two UDs using Bhattacharyya's affinity, which can be useful for comparing true vs. estimated UDs (or space use between individuals or groups):
<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE, cache=TRUE>>=
trueUD <- getUD(spatialCovs = exampleCovs,
                beta = c(-4, 6, 5, -0.1),
                log = FALSE,
                plot = FALSE)

estUD <- getUD(spatialCovs = exampleCovs,
               fit = fit,
               log = FALSE,
               plot = FALSE)

rasterOverlap(trueUD, estUD)
@
\noindent Here we can see that the estimated UD is very similar to the true UD, with a Bhattacharyya's affinity of 0.995.

\subsection{Model diagnostics}
After a model is fit using \verb|fitLangevin|, the \verb|residuals.fitLangevin| function can be utilized. This function calculates pseudo-residuals to assess model fit, evaluate assumptions, and identify potential areas of poor model performance. The \verb|plot.resLangevin| function generates Q-Q and ACF diagnostic plots for One-Step-Ahead (OSA) residuals.

\subsection{Predictions and Utilisation Distributions}
Once fitted, the \verb|predLangevin| function can be used to predict the true, unobserved continuous-time path, bridging the gap between irregular observations. The \verb|getUD| function enables the calculation of utilization distributions (UDs) representing the long-term space use of the animal based on the estimated habitat selection parameters. These can be plotted via the \verb|plotUD| function.

\subsection{Regional Probabilities and Boundary Constraints}
Understanding space use often requires calculating the probability of an animal being in a specific area. The \verb|regionProb| function calculates the probability of the animal being in a specific spatial region over time. Additionally, animals are often constrained by hard boundaries (e.g. coastlines for marine or terrestrial species). The \verb|maskLeakage| function handles boundary conditions, preventing probability mass from ``leaking'' into inaccessible areas and ensuring realistic spatial inferences.

\subsection{Simulation}
The function \verb|simLangevin| can be used to simulate movement tracks from scratch or from fitted models. Data simulation capabilities are useful for power analyses and assessing model performance.

\section{Discussion}

The \verb|langevinSSM| R package provides a robust framework for inferring continuous-time habitat selection and utilization distributions while formally accounting for the measurement error and temporal irregularity inherent in animal tracking data.

By modeling the true underlying path as a latent state variable, the state-space approach prevents the biases that arise when telemetry error is ignored. This represents an important step forward, as complex analyses requiring novel statistical methods and custom model-fitting algorithms are often not practical for many practitioners.

% there's still a lot we don't understand about the langevin model and part of the goal of langevinSSM is to allow it's properties to be further examined (e.g. raster cell size, zetaScale, discretization error)

